concepts.whyItWowsWithoutStealing:
Lando's wow isn't in his rooms: 93.1% of his elements never change a paint property. It comes from how each room hands to the next (10 seams, 9 different moves), and from the fact that when you stop, something of his is still moving. The relay puts EAS's motion in those same two places.

You're only between rooms while you're scrolling, which is when you aren't reading. So the loud moves spend their energy exactly when your attention is free: the film leaving, the ground turning to paper under the rail, the plate arriving, the card sinking while the panel comes up. When you stop to read, the words are finished and still (one wipe, fired at top 90%, never replayed).

The only thing still moving then is a baton at an edge or below a baseline, never behind or beside a sentence:
- the strip of audiences EAS builds for;
- the strip of its real clients;
- the film, which is the work itself;
- the mark writing itself once at the end.

Everything that moves belongs to EAS. Nothing is generic, the way the cursor glow is. It's one small cast restaged: the audience strip opens under the poster and closes along the footer, the way Lando's partners strip comes back in his footer.

The seams uncover and never cover, so nothing a visitor is reading ever gets slid over. And because the whole page uses one seam ledger, one wipe and one set of curves, it reads as one character instead of a collection of eight sites.

concepts.tech:
NO WebGL, NO Rive, NO NEW DEPENDENCY, NO NEW ASSET. Everything runs on components/dark/scroll-engine.ts as it stands:
- data-sp (with -lerp 0.1, -edge, -target, -step) for every scrubbed seam;
- data-bg for the one ground ramp;
- data-wipe, which already is Lando's wipe (600ms a line, 150ms stagger, bar from +300ms, fires at top 90%, once), and data-once for every other one-shot.

WHAT GETS ADDED:
(1) components/dark/strip.tsx + useStrip, one runner for all three strips:
- 4 identical clones, with JS writing the SAME translate(X%) on each (decodes §6);
- X advances at 36px/s, time-based (30px/s at ≤900px), and wraps at −100% ↔ 0 invisibly;
- direction latches to the last scroll direction, read from Lenis through the existing lenis-store.ts (it has no consumer today), or from the native scroll delta on touch, where Lenis is null;
- an optional ±10% scroll offset per strip from its own data-sp (grammar §2.13);
- one small rAF per strip, stopped when off screen (IntersectionObserver) or when document.hidden. Lando runs 8–10 small loops (decodes §11). About 2KB.
(2) The flap: a constant ellipse box scaled on Y from its top, which doesn't re-raster. Decisions 13/45 forbid clip-path or inset grows on scroll.
(3) The checkerboard: two nested custom-property transforms, both off one data-sp on the grid.
(4) Nav theme: the rail already reads the root's --flip. The plate and the panel add a sentinel pair that it reads the same way.
(5) The signature: MarkSvg's existing PEN mask, fired by data-once. MarkSvg needs per-instance ids (useId) before it mounts twice, because the nav already renders one.

PAINT BUDGET:
- colour-mix tokens step in 1/32s (decision 58); the ground doesn't step (decision 59);
- transforms and opacity only;
- the room's standing probe must hold: mean ≤10ms and 0 frames over 20ms at 1440 and 2560.

PHONE: pins already come off on touch, so the rail becomes a vertical stack with the ramp still running across it. That is exactly what Lando's phone does. Converge and checkerboard offsets drop to 0 at ≤900px (Lando's phone: no stagger). Seams, wipes and strips all run on native scroll: data-sp reads element positions, so it works on touch.

REDUCED MOTION: the engine already publishes finished frames. Strips rest at X=0, the mark rests fully written, the flap rests grown. Colour ramps still follow scroll, because colour isn't motion.

THE FIELD belongs to the always-on direction. It should follow Lando's own handling: canvas 2D rather than WebGL, device pixel ratio capped at 1.25, paused when off screen or hidden, one static frame under reduced motion. It should expose ink and amplitude inputs for the seams to set.

concepts.firstStep:
BUILD S8 · THE BOOKEND: the page's last seam and the relay's first strip.

It needs no new asset. The only standing decision it touches is the panel surface (see conflicts).

FILES: components/dark/close-section.tsx, the §06 block of app/room.css, and a new components/dark/strip.tsx. components/dark/objections-section.tsx stays untouched.

1) THE PANEL. Wrap .dr-close-in and .dr-close-foot in .dr-close-panel:
- width calc(100vw − 2·clamp(12px,1.6vw,28px)), centred (decision 53's gutter);
- border-radius clamp(22px,2.2vw,32px), the same as .dr-obj-card, so the two objects rhyme;
- overflow: clip;
- fill #0b0b0b in the day room and under paper, re-declaring the dark room's ink tokens the way .dr-zone-dark already does for the hero; on the void, var(--surface-1) plus a 1px var(--edge);
- clamp(120px,14vw,220px) of bare ground between the receding card's bottom and the panel's top (Lando: 203px at 1440);
- NO data-sp and NO transform on the panel or anything inside it, so it arrives rigid at 1:1 (Lando's ratio is 1.000 and his child offsets stay constant);
- data-nav-theme="dark", so the rail re-inks inside it.
Under paper, stop rendering .dr-paper-end, so the ground stays paper; the dark panel is the dark room from here on.

2) THE SIGNATURE. Give MarkSvg per-instance ids (useId) for its clipPath and masks. Without that, a second mount resolves url(#dr-mark-pen) to the nav's mask and would follow the nav's hover state. Then:
- swap .dr-close-mk for <MarkSvg/> at the same size (.78em of the lockup, about 92px at 1440);
- put data-once and data-once-at="0.9" on the lockup;
- .is-in runs .dr-mark-written's pen dash from 1000 to 0 over 1.1s on logopen's ease, then holds;
- no replay when scrolling back;
- under reduced motion, the mark is written at rest.

3) THE STRIP (this runner is reused later under the poster and in the voices):
- a band inside the panel, above .dr-close-util, full panel width;
- the six OFFER_ROT_WORDS at --fs-section in the panel's quietest ink, each followed by the EA mark at .7em as the separator (a mask of /brand/eas-mark.svg);
- four identical clones laid edge to edge; one rAF writes the SAME translate: X% 0 on all four;
- X advances at 36px/s (30px/s at ≤900px), converted to a percentage of one clone's width using the frame's elapsed time, and wraps at −100% ↔ 0;
- direction is the last non-zero scroll direction: onLenis from lenis-store.ts, or the native scrollY delta when Lenis is null on touch;
- NO scroll offset here, because §06 takes no scroll input, per the note in its own CSS;
- the rAF runs only while the band is on screen (IntersectionObserver) and the document isn't hidden;
- under reduced motion, X=0 and no rAF.

DONE WHEN, in both rooms and under ?v=paper, at 1440×900, 2560×1440 and 390×844:
(a) With the scroll parked at the page end, a 2s frame diff in 500ms steps shows at least 1.0% of the frame changing, and every changed pixel is inside the strip band (Lando's footer reads 1.1–1.3%). The back third goes from 0 stops moving on their own to at least 2.
(b) Strip speed measures 36±2px/s over 30s, with no visible jump at a wrap.
(c) Reversing the wheel flips its direction within one frame.
(d) The mark reads 0% written until the lockup's top crosses 0.9vh, reads 100% 1.1s later, stays at 100% through five scroll reversals, and the nav mark's hover still behaves on its own.
(e) Every child's offset from the panel's top is constant (±0.5px) at five scroll positions.
(f) The recede still measures scale 1 → 0.94 across bottom edge .6 → .3vh.
(g) The frame probe reads mean ≤10ms with 0 frames over 20ms through the recede and the panel, and the strip makes 0 rAF callbacks while the page is parked at the top.
(h) No horizontal overflow at 390, 1440 or 2560.
(i) Under paper, the ground at the page end still reads paper.

WHAT JAKE SEES: the white card sinks away and a dark panel comes up behind it. Nothing moves inside the panel except EAS's mark writing itself once. And when he stops scrolling, the audiences are still running along the bottom edge.
